A||||4||Ironhorse||Cradle bolt||||1||
Z||000000||Ironhorse||06-26-2000||09:35 PM||||Hello, it's me again.. My friend with the black fiero timing gear has stripped and we're in a process of replacing it... He had brought up a subject that the left rear cradle bolt moves around on inside the cradle.  He successfully remove it but he's worry about how to get it in.  Is there any way to weld a nut on the cradle so that it would stay and not spin around? Any suggestion please let me know TIA||||reg||1
Z||000001||zeusrekning||06-26-2000||09:42 PM||||The left rear cradle bolt was stripped in mine so we had to cut a hole in the frame and cut the thing that was in there out and weld a nut in its place? ||||reg||1
Z||000002||dman||06-26-2000||09:53 PM||||Hi Ironhorse. Hmmm...found this on the internet. Sounds like it might work for you. A few weeks back I posted here on the forum a technique to fix that pesky nut. I tried to describe the process (from memory), but I finally found the link and copied the information, unfortunately I don't remember the URL. Oh well, here's the article:<P>"BROKEN CRADLE BOLT FIX <BR>Its not uncommon for the rear cradle nut to break loose inside the frame.It cannot be reached from the other side because it is completly enclosed by the spaceframe.The nut is actually a square plate that is treaded and spotwelded to the inside of the frame.If yours ever breaks loose drill a 15/32 hole about 1/2 inch in front of the bolt hole and tap it out with a 1/2 inch course thread tap.Next cut two inches off of a half inch bolt or all-thread and cut a slot in one end for a screwdriver. Screw the two inch piece off bolt into the hole until it is flush with the space frame. The stud will keep the nut from turning. If this happens and you cannot get the old cradle bolt out. just cut the head off the bolt. drop the cradle. put the stud in the spaceframe next to the nut and remove the old bolt."<P>Since I haven't had any cradle bolts break on me I (thankfully) haven't had the opportunity to test this method, therefore I can't guarantee that it will work. But if it does it sure beats cutting out a small section of the frame.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||06-26-2000||10:40 PM||||when i r/r the engine on my GT, the right rear cradle bolt moved around a lot.  i was really worried about this, and did not want to cut anything.  i was prepared to drill and insert a new bolt to hold the nut... however, after getting the other 3 bolts in place, this somehow placed soem tension on the nut/bolt to allow me to tighten it down, without it spinning!<P><BR>just a thought.<BR>hoop<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||TheFiero64||06-27-2000||11:52 AM||||Both of mine came loose. I lifted the body and let the wait of the cradle push on the bolts. It seemed to hold tight enough to stop the nut from spinning inside. I kept my fingers crossed as I threaded them back in. I have dropped the cradle twice more after this and have had no problems doing it this way.||||reg||3
